# Number keys and fitting the view

Outside a constrained transform, the top number row and numpad have separate
view bindings. When a Move, Rotate, or Scale axis is locked for a selection,
number keys enter an [exact transform amount](numeric-transforms.md) instead.
Finish or cancel that session to use the view shortcuts again.

| View        | Top row                       | Numpad                          |
| ----------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Perspective | {{shortcut:view.perspective}} | {{shortcut:numpad.perspective}} |
| Front       | {{shortcut:view.front}}       | {{shortcut:numpad.front}}       |
| Right       | {{shortcut:view.right}}       | {{shortcut:numpad.right}}       |
| Top         | {{shortcut:view.top}}         | {{shortcut:numpad.top}}         |
| Back        | {{shortcut:view.back}}        | —                               |
| Left        | {{shortcut:view.left}}        | —                               |
| Bottom      | {{shortcut:view.bottom}}      | —                               |

The dash means no direct preset binding in that column. All six orthographic directions
are available in {{control:view}}, including {{control:view.back}},
{{control:view.left}}, and {{control:view.bottom}}.
{{shortcut:view.projection}} on the top row toggles perspective and orthographic
projection without changing the viewing direction; {{shortcut:view.perspective}}
selects the Perspective view preset.

Settled, aligned orthographic views also show the [2D ruler](2d-ruler.md)
along the top and left of the viewport.

{{shortcut:numpad.orbit-left}} / {{shortcut:numpad.orbit-right}} orbit left / right,
and {{shortcut:numpad.orbit-down}} / {{shortcut:numpad.orbit-up}} orbit down / up by
**{{value:orbit-degrees}}°**. {{shortcut:numpad.projection}} toggles perspective and orthographic
projection without changing the viewing direction.

Use {{shortcut:view.frame}} to fit the whole document, just like {{control:frame}} in {{control:view}}. It restores the default viewing angle and retains the
current projection mode.

Use {{shortcut:view.frame-selection}} to center and fit the current selection. In
object mode, it includes every selected object and preserves your viewing
direction and projection. You can also right-click {{control:viewport}} and
choose {{control:frame-selection}}.

{{image:view-keys-object-fit}}

In vertex edit mode, {{shortcut:view.frame-selection}} fits the selected vertices. A single selected
vertex is centered without changing the existing zoom. With nothing selected,
the view stays unchanged.

{{image:view-keys-vertex-fit}}

These view controls keep your geometry and selection unchanged and create no
undo steps. Text fields and menus retain their own keyboard controls.

See [navigation](navigation.md) or [selecting with keys](selection-keys.md).
[Back to guide](README.md).
